```
$ jci-coverage complexity --help
Computes the cyclomatic complexity of the Rust functions a Cobertura
report covers and writes it into each method, where OtterWise reads it
for its Complexity and CRAP scores. `cargo llvm-cov` leaves every
complexity at 0. Reads the source files the report names, relative to
`--source-root`, and needs no other tool. Run it after `report` has
written a Cobertura report, and before `upload`.

Usage: jci-coverage complexity [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --file <PATH>
          Cobertura report to annotate.
          
          The Cobertura file `report` writes.
          
          [default: coverage/cobertura.xml]

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase logging verbosity

      --output <PATH>
          Write the annotated report here instead of over `--file`.
          
          The default replaces the report in place; a failed run never
          leaves a half-written file.

  -q, --quiet...
          Decrease logging verbosity

      --source-root <DIR>
          Directory the report's source file paths are relative to
          
          [default: .]

      --duplicates <DUPLICATES>
          What to do with the several entries `cargo llvm-cov` writes
          for one generic function

          Possible values:
          - merge: One entry per function: complexity is a property of
            the source, so it should not depend on how many
            instantiations the tests happen to make
          - keep:  Leave every instantiation as `cargo llvm-cov` wrote
            it. OtterWise sums every entry, so a generic function
            counts once per instantiation
          
          [default: merge]

      --rules <RULES>
          Which decision points count

          Possible values:
          - rca:    Matches `rust-code-analysis`: every match arm and
            every `?` counts
          - mccabe: Textbook McCabe: a `match` adds one less than its
            arms, and `?` is not a decision
          
          [default: rca]

      --unmatched <UNMATCHED>
          What to do with a method that has no matching function in
          the source

          Possible values:
          - keep: Leave its complexity at `0`
          - one:  Count it as `1`, the complexity of a function with
            no decisions
          - fail: Fail the run
          
          [default: keep]

      --no-rollup
          Leave the class, package and root complexity as they are.
          
          By default they are set to the sum of the methods below
          them, so other Cobertura consumers see consistent data.
          OtterWise ignores them.

      --exclude-tests
          Count `#[cfg(test)]` code and `#[test]` functions as
          complexity 0

      --dry-run
          Work out and print the summary without writing anything

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```
